{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"12327ee70dc20b8f","name":"card_unit_p_39_airacobra","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/WorldAtWar/units/card_unit_p_39_airacobra","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"LITTLE COBRA","text":"Deployment: Destroy another random friendly unit.","flavorText":"The Bell P-39 Airacobra was one of the most unusual American fighters of WWII, featuring a mid-mounted engine and tricycle landing gear. While the US and British air forces found it underwhelming at high altitudes, the Soviet Union turned it into a formidable low-altitude weapon. Delivered through the Lend-Lease program, over 4,700 P-39s were sent to the USSR, making it one of the most-used American aircraft on the Eastern Front. Soviet pilots valued the P-39’s durability, firepower, and performance at low to medium altitudes, where most of their air combat took place. Its 37 mm cannon, firing through the propeller hub, proved deadly against both aircraft and lightly armored ground targets. Ace pilots like Aleksandr Pokryshkin and Grigori Rechkalov achieved dozens of aerial victories flying Airacobras. Unlike their Western allies, the Soviets flew the P-39 aggressively in frontline roles well into 1945. Though often dismissed in the West, the P-39 became a workhorse in Soviet hands and contributed significantly to the air war on the Eastern Front.","faction":"Soviet","type":"fighter","rarity":"Rare","cost":3,"operationCost":2,"attack":4,"defense":5},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_p_39_airacobra","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_unit_p_39_airacobra_title","SourceString":"LITTLE COBRA","LocalizedString":"LITTLE COBRA"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":4,"defense":5,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_unit_p_39_airacobra_text","SourceString":"Deployment: Destroy another random friendly unit.","LocalizedString":"Deployment: Destroy another random friendly unit."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_unit_p_39_airacobra_history","SourceString":"The Bell P-39 Airacobra was one of the most unusual American fighters of WWII, featuring a mid-mounted engine and tricycle landing gear. While the US and British air forces found it underwhelming at high altitudes, the Soviet Union turned it into a formidable low-altitude weapon. Delivered through the Lend-Lease program, over 4,700 P-39s were sent to the USSR, making it one of the most-used American aircraft on the Eastern Front. Soviet pilots valued the P-39’s durability, firepower, and performance at low to medium altitudes, where most of their air combat took place. Its 37 mm cannon, firing through the propeller hub, proved deadly against both aircraft and lightly armored ground targets. Ace pilots like Aleksandr Pokryshkin and Grigori Rechkalov achieved dozens of aerial victories flying Airacobras. Unlike their Western allies, the Soviets flew the P-39 aggressively in frontline roles well into 1945. Though often dismissed in the West, the P-39 became a workhorse in Soviet hands and contributed significantly to the air war on the Eastern Front.","LocalizedString":"The Bell P-39 Airacobra was one of the most unusual American fighters of WWII, featuring a mid-mounted engine and tricycle landing gear. While the US and British air forces found it underwhelming at high altitudes, the Soviet Union turned it into a formidable low-altitude weapon. Delivered through the Lend-Lease program, over 4,700 P-39s were sent to the USSR, making it one of the most-used American aircraft on the Eastern Front. Soviet pilots valued the P-39’s durability, firepower, and performance at low to medium altitudes, where most of their air combat took place. Its 37 mm cannon, firing through the propeller hub, proved deadly against both aircraft and lightly armored ground targets. Ace pilots like Aleksandr Pokryshkin and Grigori Rechkalov achieved dozens of aerial victories flying Airacobras. Unlike their Western allies, the Soviets flew the P-39 aggressively in frontline roles well into 1945. Though often dismissed in the West, the P-39 became a workhorse in Soviet hands and contributed significantly to the air war on the Eastern Front."},"operationCost":2,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","hasDeployment":true,"hasBlitz":true,"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Soviet/World_at_War/T_p39_airacobra.T_p39_airacobra","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Jaroslaw Wróbel","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/P-39_AIRACOBRA.P-39_AIRACOBRA","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":321.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_unit_p_39_airacobra_photo","SourceString":"P-39 Airacobras awaiting flight to the Soviet Union. Date unknown.","LocalizedString":"P-39 Airacobras awaiting flight to the Soviet Union. Date unknown."},"blowUpExplosion":{"ObjectName":"ParticleSystem'P_ExplosionAircraftL'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Explosions/Large/Aircraft/P_ExplosionAircraftL.201"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeHeavy_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeHeavy.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackFighter.BP_AttackFighter_C","SubPathString":""},"isDraw":true},"variables":[{"name":"possibleUnits","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_unit_p_39_airacobra","owner":"card_unit_p_39_airacobra_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"K2Node_Event_targetCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"